.TH SCAN_VARINT 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
scan_varint, scan_pb_type0_int \- Scan a variable-length integer from a string

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/scan.h>

.IB size_t
.IB scan_varint(const char* in, size_t len, uint64* n);

.IB size_t
.IB scan_pb_type0_int(const char* dest, size_t len, uint64* l);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBscan_varint\fR function scans a variable-length integer from a given string and stores the result in the location pointed to by \fBn\fR. Variable-length integers are commonly used in Protocol Buffers and other binary data formats. The function reads up to \fBlen\fR bytes from the input string starting at \fBin\fR and decodes the integer.

The \fBscan_pb_type0_int\fR function is an alias for \fBscan_varint\fR, providing compatibility with Protocol Buffers (PB) type 0.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB in/dest
The input string containing the variable-length integer.
.TP
.IB len
The maximum number of characters to read from the input string.
.TP
.IB n/l
The destination where the scanned variable-length integer will be stored.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of characters processed. If the input string is empty or does not contain a valid variable-length integer, the function returns 0.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBscan_varint\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/scan.h>

int main() {
    const char* input = "\x08\x96\x01";  # 123 as a varint
    size_t len = 3;
    uint64 result;
    size_t processed = scan_varint(input, len, &result);
    printf("Scanned variable-length integer: %llu\n", result);
    printf("Processed characters: %zu\n", processed);
    return 0;
}
.nef